export const body = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Fund accounting is the controlled record-keeping and reporting work that supports pooled investment vehicles such as mutual funds. It is different from choosing investments or giving investment advice. The focus is on accurate records, valuations, cash and position checks, expenses, unit activity, and the information used to report a fund's value. The exact systems, approvals, deadlines, products, and hand-offs vary by fund, service provider, jurisdiction, and employer.\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"For a graduate exploring investment operations, fund accounting is a useful way to learn how accounting, reconciliation, controls, and communication fit together. This guide uses a simplified learning workflow rather than an employer-specific procedure. It does not tell anyone what to invest in, how to value a particular security, or how to perform a regulated calculation without an organisation's current policy and supervision.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"What fund accounting means\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"A fund brings together assets, liabilities, income, expenses, and unit-holder activity into a controlled accounting view. The work may include receiving source data, checking that records agree, following valuation inputs approved under the relevant policy, recording activity, investigating breaks, and preparing outputs for review. In practice, the work is collaborative: operations, custody, pricing, compliance, technology, and review teams can each own part of the process.\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"In the Indian mutual-fund context, the Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI) explains net asset value (NAV) as the per-unit value of a scheme after liabilities are considered. Its investor material also explains that NAV is based on net assets divided by outstanding units. Those definitions help a learner understand the objective of the workflow; they are not a substitute for current valuation policies, fund documents, or professional review.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Read SEBI's investor explanation of net asset value\",\"href\":\"https://investor.sebi.gov.in/securities-mf-investments.html\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Review SEBI's mutual fund FAQ on NAV\",\"href\":\"https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/faqfiles/sep-2024/1727242783639.pdf\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"A simplified NAV workflow\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"NAV is often introduced with a simple formula: net assets divided by the number of outstanding units. The formula is useful, but the disciplined work happens before a figure is released. A real operating procedure may include product-specific rules, cut-off times, maker-checker controls, exception queues, approvals, and documented valuation methodology. The sequence below is a learning model, not a universal daily process.\"},{\"type\":\"list\",\"ordered\":true,\"items\":[\"Collect the approved records for cash, holdings, trades, corporate actions, income, expenses, and unit activity.\",\"Check completeness, dates, identifiers, and whether records from relevant sources agree before treating them as ready for processing.\",\"Apply the organisation's approved valuation inputs and accounting treatment; do not invent a value to make a report balance.\",\"Investigate differences, record the evidence and next action, then escalate exceptions that need another team's authority or information.\",\"Calculate or prepare the net-assets and per-unit output in accordance with the applicable process, followed by required review and release controls.\",\"Keep an auditable record of the source, checks performed, exceptions, approvals, and final status.\"]},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Reconciliation is especially important in this kind of work. When two records differ, the right response is to understand the difference, preserve the evidence, and follow the escalation process. Forcing a match or overwriting source data may hide a real issue.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Learn how reconciliation works in finance\",\"href\":\"/resources/reconciliation-in-finance/\",\"routeId\":\"resource-reconciliation-in-finance\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Core skills for fund accounting roles\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":3,\"text\":\"Accounting and financial terminology\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"A beginner should be comfortable with the relationship between assets, liabilities, income, expenses, and units. You do not need to claim expert knowledge of every product. It is more useful to explain a simple concept accurately, identify what you would need to confirm, and know when a policy or specialist review is required.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":3,\"text\":\"Data checks and Excel discipline\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Spreadsheet skills are valuable for organising data, comparing fields, highlighting exceptions, and keeping a clear calculation trail. Practise sorting, filtering, lookups, conditional checks, pivot tables, version control, and clear file naming. Accuracy is more important than using complex formulas. Keep source data separate from working calculations and document each adjustment or assumption.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":3,\"text\":\"Controls, investigation, and communication\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"A strong analyst can state what the record shows, what was checked, what remains unresolved, and who should take the next action. Practise writing concise exception notes that distinguish facts from assumptions. Be careful with confidential information: use only fictional or authorised data in practice work, resumes, portfolios, and interview examples.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Explore the investment banking operations career guide\",\"href\":\"/career-guides/investment-banking-operations/\",\"routeId\":\"career-guide-investment-banking-operations\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Build a safe practice project\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"A fictional mini-project can demonstrate operations thinking without exposing private information. Create a small workbook with invented holdings, cash balances, expenses, liabilities, and units. Then create a second fictional source report containing a few deliberate differences. Your goal is not to create a production NAV; it is to show that you can organise information, compare records, explain a break, and document a controlled next step.\"},{\"type\":\"list\",\"ordered\":true,\"items\":[\"Draw a one-page flow from source data to review, using your own words.\",\"Create an invented holdings and cash file with clear IDs, dates, and source labels.\",\"Build a separate working sheet that checks totals and highlights fictional exceptions.\",\"Write an exception log with the issue, evidence, owner, status, and escalation point for each difference.\",\"Prepare a two-minute explanation of how you protected source data and why you did not force unmatched records to agree.\"]},{\"type\":\"callout\",\"title\":\"Use fictional data only\",\"text\":\"Never use client, employer, fund, account, or confidential system data in a public portfolio. Employers can assess your judgement from a realistic fictional example and a clear explanation of the controls you would follow.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"NAV inputs and control questions\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"The simple net-assets-divided-by-units formula is only the final relationship. A controlled workflow needs complete holdings and cash, approved prices or valuation inputs, income, expenses, liabilities, subscriptions, redemptions, corporate actions, foreign-exchange inputs where relevant, and the correct unit balance. Each input has a source, date, owner, and review requirement. A learner should ask what the number represents and how it was evidenced before using it in a calculation.\"},{\"type\":\"list\",\"items\":[\"Scope: which fund, share class or plan, valuation date, currency, and accounting period are being processed?\",\"Completeness: did every expected position, cash account, trade, income event, expense, liability, and unit movement arrive?\",\"Valuation: which approved price or method applies, and how are stale, missing, or exceptional inputs handled?\",\"Reconciliation: do custody, accounting, cash, trade, and unit records agree for the defined scope?\",\"Review: which tolerances, materiality rules, maker-checker steps, and release approvals apply?\",\"Evidence: can a reviewer trace the final output back to sources, exceptions, decisions, and approvals?\"]},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Fictional NAV exception example\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Assume a fictional fund-accounting file contains 1,000 units of a security, while the approved custodian position file contains 900. The difference should not be removed merely because the previous day's holding was 1,000. The analyst confirms the security and account identifiers, checks the trade and settlement records, reviews corporate-action activity, compares file dates and versions, and records the first point at which the position changed.\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Suppose the evidence shows that a sale of 100 units settled before the valuation cut-off but the internal position file did not update. The responsible team confirms the authorised correction, the revised file is independently reviewed, and the position and resulting calculation are rechecked. If the evidence remains incomplete, the item stays open and follows the escalation and valuation policy. The example teaches investigation; it does not prescribe a real fund's accounting or valuation treatment.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Fund accounting interview questions to practise\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"For a dated employer example, a Citi Fund Accounting Analyst 1 listing in Haryana posted 4 September 2026 describes mutual-fund financial reporting, accuracy and completeness checks, audit work papers, Excel and client deadlines. Despite its Analyst 1 title, that specific posting asks for 3–4 years of relevant experience. It illustrates why graduates must read the actual experience rule and team output instead of assuming an analyst title is entry level. The posting may close or change and this article is not a vacancy listing.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Review the dated Citi fund-accounting role example\",\"href\":\"https://jobs.citi.com/job/haryana/fund-accounting-analyst-1/287/99795551184\"},{\"type\":\"list\",\"items\":[\"Explain NAV at a high level and identify the main inputs that require review.\",\"What would you do if a custody position and an accounting position differ?\",\"How would a late trade, expense, corporate action, or cash movement affect the workflow?\",\"Why can a matched total still contain a control or classification problem?\",\"How would you document an exception so that another analyst can continue the investigation?\",\"What information must remain confidential in a portfolio or interview example?\"]},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"A strong answer separates the definition from the employer procedure. Start with the purpose, state the records and checks, identify what you would not assume, and finish with ownership, escalation, and closure evidence. Freshers can demonstrate this reasoning with fictional data without claiming production experience.\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Where fund accounting fits in investment operations\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Fund accounting is one of several operations areas connected with investment products. Investment banking operations can also include trade settlement, reconciliation, corporate actions, and record controls. The product, team structure, system access, and client model determine what a particular role covers, so read every job description closely instead of assuming that titles mean the same thing at every employer.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Follow the trade lifecycle from capture to reconciliation\",\"href\":\"/career-guides/trade-lifecycle/\",\"routeId\":\"career-guide-trade-lifecycle\"},{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"text\":\"Centaur Careers presents fund accounting alongside settlements, reconciliation, and corporate actions within its Investment Banking Operations learning area. Use the programme page to understand the published course scope, and confirm current cohort details directly with the team before making an enrolment decision.\"},{\"type\":\"link\",\"label\":\"Explore the Investment Banking Operations learning area\",\"href\":\"/courses/investment-banking-operations/\",\"routeId\":\"investment-banking-operations\"},{\"type\":\"heading\",\"level\":2,\"text\":\"Frequently asked questions\"},{\"type\":\"faq\",\"question\":\"Is fund accounting the same as investment analysis?\",\"answer\":\"No. Fund accounting focuses on controlled records, calculations, checks, reporting, and supporting workflows. Investment analysis and portfolio decision-making are separate functions, although roles may work with related information.\"},{\"type\":\"faq\",\"question\":\"How is NAV explained simply?\",\"answer\":\"At a high level, NAV is the fund's net assets divided by the outstanding units on the valuation date. The detailed inputs, valuation rules, timing, review, and disclosure process depend on the fund and its applicable policies.\"},{\"type\":\"faq\",\"question\":\"Can a graduate prepare for a fund accounting role?\",\"answer\":\"A graduate can build relevant foundations through accounting basics, financial terminology, spreadsheet discipline, reconciliation practice, documentation, and clear communication. Employers set their own eligibility and experience criteria, so review each job description carefully.\"}]");
